o-letter ending that goes with it\\\".\\n    # And \\\"The three teens that sit between ten and fourteen are the well-known exception and take the plain ending.\\\"\\n    # This is confusing. Let's re-read.\\n    # \\\"Write a positive whole number the way it is said when counting positions\\\" -> Ordinal numbers?\\n    # 1st, 2nd, 3rd...\\n    # \\\"the number itself followed by the two-letter ending that goes with it\\\" -> 1st, 2nd, 3rd.\\n    # \\\"The three teens that sit between ten and fourteen are the well-known exception and take the plain ending.\\\"\\n    # Between 10 and 14 are 11, 12, 13.\\n    # So 11th, 12th, 13th? No, \\\"take the plain ending\\\".\\n    # Maybe it means they don't have a suffix? Or they have a different one?\\n    # Wait, if I count positions: 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th...\\n    # The teens between 10 and 14 are 11, 12, 13.\\n    # \\\"take the plain ending\\\" might mean they don't have a suffix? Or just 'th'?\\n    # Actually, in some contexts (like dates or specific counting), maybe it's different.\\n    pass\\n\\n# Let's look for similar problems online. This sounds like a coding challenge.\\n# \\\"Write a positive whole number the way it is said when counting positions\\\"\\n# Maybe it's about ordinal numbers in English?\\n# 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, 5th, 6th, 7th, 8th, 9th, 10th, 11th, 12th, 13th, 14th...\\n# \\\"The three teens that sit between ten and fourteen are the well-known exception and take the plain ending.\\\"\\n# This is very specific. If they were 11th, 12th, 13th, they would have 'th'.\\n# Maybe it means they don't have a suffix? \\\"eleven\\\", \\\"twelve\\\", \\\"thirteen\\\"?\\n# But then what about 14? It says \\\"between ten and fourteen\\\". So 14 is not an exception.\\n# 14th.\\n\\n# Let's try to think if there's another interpretation.\\n# Maybe it's about the suffix of the number itself?\\n# 1st, 2nd, 3rd...\\n# If n=1: \\\"1st\\\"\\n# If n=2: \\\"2nd\\\"\\n# If n=3: \\\"3rd\\\"\\n# If n=4: \\\"4th\\\"\\n# ...\\n# If 